// Full-coverage typecheck for the WebUI package.
//
// This program holds every test file and every source file in the package to
// `strict`. It replaces a narrower program that named four test files out of the
// hundred-odd in `test/unit/`, so the rest — and the client components they
// render — were not type-checked by anything in the standard gate sequence. A
// test can call a function with the wrong arity, or pass a string where a
// discriminated union is expected, and still pass: Vitest's transform strips
// types without checking them. `pnpm verify` was the only gate that ever saw
// those files, through the separate `test-full` program this one absorbed.
//
// It extends `tsconfig.json` rather than `tsconfig.server.json`, because this is
// a client-side program: it renders through `react-dom/server` and holds the
// browser tree. The overrides, each for a specific error class rather than for
// convenience:
//
//   * `lib: ES2023` — `Array.prototype.findLastIndex` needs the newer lib.
//   * `DOM`/`DOM.Iterable` — the test tree renders DOM nodes server-side.
//   * `module: ESNext` + `moduleResolution: Bundler` — the resolution the
//     bundler actually uses. The inherited `NodeNext` pair bound the
//     `lottie-web` default import to the CJS namespace, so `loadAnimation`
//     appeared missing, and demanded a `type: "json"` attribute on the
//     animation JSON import.
//   * `jsx: react-jsx` — the test tree contains `.tsx` files, and
//     `tsconfig.server.json` inherits `jsx: preserve`, which leaves `JSX.Element`
//     unresolved.
//   * `allowImportingTsExtensions` — the path map points at `.ts` entry points.
//
// Neither sibling program is lost. `tsconfig.server.json` still holds the server
// and runtime trees under `NodeNext`, and `tsconfig.client.json` still holds the
// client tree under the bundler; this one checks both trees together under the
// bundler, because a test imports both at once.
//
// Two strictness flags are *not* in force here, and never were:
// `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` and `noImplicitOverride` are turned off in
// `tsconfig.json`, because this program's path map pulls in workspace packages
// and vendored `third_party` sources that are written to a looser standard and
// TypeScript has no per-directory options. `strict` and `verbatimModuleSyntax`
// do apply, and those are the two that catch the errors this program exists for.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "lib": ["ES2023", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true
  },
  "include": [
    "test/**/*.ts",
    "test/**/*.tsx",
    "src/client/**/*.ts",
    "src/client/**/*.tsx",
    "src/shared/**/*.ts",
    "src/server/**/*.ts",
    "src/runtime/**/*.ts",
    "vite.config.ts"
  ]
}
